kernel/build/

# Generated expected-dword tables for the sovereign-encoder oracle (scripts/check/shader-tables.sh).
# ⛔ NEVER COMMIT THESE. They are a machine-readable view of the committed hex in kernel/core/gpu.cyr,
# regenerated by host-gpu-oracles.sh on every run. A committed copy would go stale and let shaderasm
# compare a new emit list against an old shader and pass — the same failure as the committed
# tests/gpu/build/ binaries, which is how a broken oracle stayed green for months.
tests/gpu/gen/

# Host oracle + agnos-target tool builds. ⛔ NEVER COMMIT THESE — 51 of them were tracked until
# 1.56.44, and that is precisely how a broken oracle stayed green for months. `tests/gpu/build/edgeasm`
# was committed at 108,784 B; running it exited 95 and printed "B4 PASS -- the tool reproduces a
# shipped iron-proven shader byte-for-byte", while `edgeasm.cyr` beside it COULD NOT COMPILE AT ALL
# (its include resolved to `<agnos>/mabda`, a directory that has never existed). Anyone who verified
# the oracle by running the binary got a green light from a fossil. A committed build artifact is not
# evidence about the source beside it; it is evidence about whatever source existed when someone last
# ran a compiler — and unlike a stale doc, it PASSES, because a stale oracle does not fail, it agrees.
#
# Nothing needs these committed, and it was checked rather than assumed:
#   · the 15 host oracles are REBUILT by host-gpu-oracles.sh before every run (that loop's own header
#     forbids "optimising" it into reusing an existing build/<t>) — a committed copy is never read;
#   · the 11 `*_agnos` tools that DO have a consumer (stage-tools.sh -> build/rootfs/bin -> the burn)
#     now build-if-absent for `agnos/*` rows, so their absence produces a BUILD, not a fossil;
#   · `modeset-tool-smoke.sh` already rebuilt `modeset_agnos` itself before using it;
#   · all 11 rebuilt BYTE-IDENTICAL to the committed copies, so tracking them carried no information.
# ⚠ Four of the 51 — au, gpublit_cmp, inc_probe, szprobe — had NO .cyr source anywhere in the tree
# and no reference from any script or doc: binaries the repo could not regenerate even in principle.
#
# ⛔⛔ WIDENED gpu -> * ON 2026-08-24, BECAUSE THE 1.56.44 PURGE ONLY SWEPT ONE OF SEVEN DIRECTORIES.
# The rule above was written as `tests/gpu/build/` and TWELVE fossils survived it in the other six:
#   tests/audio/build/{tonegen,tonegen_agnos}  tests/blk/build/{blkprobe,blkwr,gptwr}
#   tests/chan/build/{chantest,chanx,ptyhost,ptyx}  tests/fault/build/faulter_agnos
#   tests/fp/build/fpex  tests/symlink/build/symtest
# ⚠ AND THEY WERE NOT BYTE-IDENTICAL, which is what separates this from the gpu case. Rebuilt at the
# 6.5.28 pin, every one measured differed from its committed copy — symtest 13,856 B committed vs
# 18,552 B built, gptwr 93,368 vs 97,880, blkprobe/blkwr 17,920 vs 18,328. So the 11 gpu tools'
# "tracking them carried no information" did NOT hold here: these carried WRONG information, from a
# toolchain nobody can now name.
# ⚠⚠ WORSE, ONE OF THEM DEFEATED THE VERY FIX THE NOTE ABOVE DESCRIBES. stage_one's build-if-absent
# (scripts/burn/stage-tools.sh) fires on `[ -f "$bin" ] || _autobuild=1` — a TRACKED fossil is always
# present, so `faulter_agnos` and `tonegen_agnos` could never take that path. The repair the gpu purge
# shipped was inert for the two in-tree rows outside tests/gpu; it only LOOKED complete because the
# ignore beside it stopped at one directory. ⇒ The gitignore rule and the auto-build repair have to
# cover the same set, or the second is a comment about the first.
# ⚠ `tests/chan/build/{ptyhost,ptyx}` had the one real consumer that read a binary WITHOUT building it
# (scripts/harness/pty-host-test.py); it now builds-if-absent, mirroring stage_one. `tonegen_agnos`
# had no consumer at all.
tests/*/build/

# Vendored stdlib snapshots. ⛔ NEVER COMMIT THESE — same argument as the build artifacts above, one
# layer down: `cyrius deps` (which `cyrius build` runs BY DEFAULT, no flag) rewrites tests/*/lib/ from
# the pinned toolchain on every single build, so a committed copy is overwritten before it is ever
# read. It cannot influence a build; it can only produce diff noise and misinform a reader.
#
# ⛔ AND IT DID BOTH (found 2026-08-24). tests/symlink/lib/ and tests/blk/lib/ held 7 syscalls*.cyr
# each that matched NO installed snapshot — not the 6.5.28 pin, not the 6.5.27 it had just moved off,
# not the 6.5.35 on the dev box. Both predate v6.4.51 (no `signal_ignore`, no `signal_default`), so
# they had been fossils across dozens of releases. Last touched by "1.51.0 work" and "move folders".
# ⚠ The FIVE other dirs matched 6.5.28 exactly — which is the trap: staleness here is silent and
# per-directory, and the two that had drifted were indistinguishable from the five that had not
# without diffing all 88 files against ~/.cyrius/versions/. Nothing in the tree did that.
# ⚠ .gitignore's `/lib/` (line 9) is ROOT-ANCHORED, so it never covered these. That is the only
# reason 88 vendored files were tracked at all — an omission, never a decision.
#
# Nothing needs them committed, and it was checked rather than assumed:
#   · every tests/* manifest declares `[deps] stdlib = [...]` ONLY — no `[deps.NAME]` git deps — so
#     resolution copies from ~/.cyrius/versions/<pin>/lib/, a LOCAL path. Verified by building
#     tests/symlink with lib/ deleted under `unshare -rn` (no network at all): it succeeded and
#     repopulated all 7 files byte-identical to the 6.5.28 pin.
#   · no script or CI step builds a tests/* dir with `--no-deps`; every one (host-gpu-oracles.sh,
#     the smoke scripts, stage-tools.sh) uses a plain `cyrius build`, which resolves deps first.
#   · `--no-deps` would not read these anyway — it skips the stdlib include INJECTION entirely, so
#     tests/symlink fails `undefined function 'sys_symlink'` with a FRESH lib/ just as with a stale
#     one. There is no build mode in which a committed snapshot is the thing that gets compiled.
#   · no cyrius.lock is written for stdlib-only manifests (the lock covers `[deps.NAME]` git deps),
#     so untracking lib/ strands nothing — confirmed, no lock appears after a tests/* build.
tests/*/lib/

# Python bytecode from the scripts/harness/*.py boot harnesses. Untracked noise otherwise —
# every harness run leaves one, and it shows up in `git status` beside real work.
__pycache__/
